WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.535
-We're back with my family,
everyone,

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.471
and like I said, it's so nice
to be together again

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.638
and continue this tradition.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.041
Another tradition
is that every year

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:10.009
we have dressed up
first my son Ashe,

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.178
and then when he was born,
my son Ashe and Axel

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:15.281
and our dog, Frisbee,
as turkeys.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.082
This is something
we're going to continue to do

00:00:17.082 --> 00:00:18.718
until they get too old

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.152
where they don't want
to do it anymore.

00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:21.621
-Yeah, about 20, 22.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:22.989
-Yeah.

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.224
-The dog has never
really been into it.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:27.894
-No, and, in fact, keep an eye
out for a couple things.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:31.798
One, do you think the dog
likes being in this video?

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:33.199
And, two, keep your eye out.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.634
If you peel your eyes
very closely,

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:39.138
there's sort of an Easter egg
that maybe you will find.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:41.674
But, again, watch very closely.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.477
Here are the boys and Frisbee
as turkeys.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.514
-Boggle, boggle, boggle!
Thanksgiving!

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:51.083
[ Giggling ]

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.386
-Boggle, boggle, boggle!

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:56.155
Happy Thanksgiving!

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:58.758
One, two, three. Surprise!

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.894
[ Audience awws ]

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:03.529
[ Applause ]

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:06.399
♪♪

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:10.002
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:13.706
-We had another one, you guys!
We had another one!

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.875
[ Applause ]

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.510
This is --

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:22.915
10 weeks ago, my wife gave birth
to a baby girl, and...

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:24.784
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.086
I feel like
it's only fitting for you

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:29.055
to tell everybody
what her name is.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:31.023
-Oh, my gosh.
Her name is Adelaide.

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:32.825
Addie, after my mother.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:34.227
-Yes.
-Which --

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:37.029
So, I knew,
knowing Seth and Alexi,

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:40.399
it had to start with an "A,"
it had to be a cool name.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:42.701
And I know what family
means to all of them.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:46.472
So I thought I pretty much
had it, but I didn't know.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.307
And I didn't want to ask.
-Yep.

00:01:48.307 --> 00:01:49.575
-And so it waited.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.712
You waited until we were
on the vineyard. Something.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.046
It was a rainy day.

00:01:54.046 --> 00:01:56.849
And then you said,
"Do you want to know the name?"

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.084
And I said,
"I guess so. Yes."

00:01:59.084 --> 00:02:00.686
And then, of course,
I burst into tears.

00:02:00.686 --> 00:02:04.423
-You burst into tears. And, now,
Dad, had it been a boy,

00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:06.459
what was the name
that you were arguing for?

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:09.496
-Albert.
-And do you want to explain

00:02:09.496 --> 00:02:10.963
what our hesitation
would have been

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:12.665
to naming a child Albert?

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.634
-I've had six dogs named Albert.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:15.835
-Yeah.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:22.275
-Um, I currently have
Albert VI...

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.410
but I thought it would
have been a good name

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.946
because it would have been like,
you know --

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:31.250
I've had -- Five of my Alberts
have died.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.886
They had good lives.
Don't worry.

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:36.022
But I thought it would be
like Pinocchio, you know?

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:39.025
And all of a sudden,
the dog turns into a kid.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:43.296
They went and screwed it up
having a girl.

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.132
-Yeah. I, um -- I do --

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:49.435
I only -- You know,
I'm so happy to have a girl.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.071
It was a dream come true
to have a girl,

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.007
but I only wish
it had been a boy

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:58.211
just for you to explain
to my wife,

00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.747
"It's like the dog
came back as a kid."

00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:02.682
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:05.150
-Yeah.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:09.889
It could have gone better
than we all think.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:12.625
-Now, Addie had
a drink of choice.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.193
Posh, do you remember
what Addie drank?

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:16.829
-Kir Royale.
-Yes, this is --

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:20.667
Not the baby Addie,
but the dear departed Addie.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.302
And so I would like
to take a toast

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:26.406
in memory of
our grandmother and --

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:28.107
-My mother.
-Your mother.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.877
And, Dad, your arch nemesis.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.011
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.011 --> 00:03:35.348
-Cheers.
-To Addie.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:37.149
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:37.149 --> 00:03:41.587
-And if I could just interject
something about Kirs.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.089
Now, it's white wine
with crème de cassis.

00:03:44.089 --> 00:03:47.159
The Royale is with champagne,
as well.

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:50.363
But my mother had a flask
that she carried with her

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:53.666
at all times
that had the crème de cassis

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:55.568
because she says
it's such a rip-off

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:58.137
when you go to restaurants,
so a regular glass of white wine

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:01.774
is maybe $6,
then they jack it up to 10

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.643
just by putting in
the crème de cassis.

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:06.845
And she says half the time, it's
too red, it's not red enough.

00:04:06.845 --> 00:04:08.347
She said,
"I'm just gonna do my own."

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:11.750
So she always took along
this flask and would pour it.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.286
Now, the thing is, with crème
de cassis, it's, like, a real

00:04:14.286 --> 00:04:15.787
gooey, red...

00:04:15.787 --> 00:04:17.623
-Yeah, very thick.
-Very thick.

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:20.893
-So, one time, the thing broke.
The lid came off.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.629
We were in a store shopping.
And I looked down,

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:26.332
and it was, like,
one of those straw rugs,

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.600
and there was crème de --

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.836
It looked like somebody
had been murdered.

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:31.403
-Yeah.
-And there was --

00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:32.938
There was just red all the way,

00:04:32.938 --> 00:04:34.841
and I looked at Mother,
and she said,

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:37.910
"We need to leave right now."
-Yeah.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.212
-So we got out. We got out.

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:43.082
-The point of that story is she
didn't only carry that flask

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.450
when she went shopping.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.652
-Yeah.
-Or to the restaurant.

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:49.421
Always had it.
-It's very funny to think of...

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:52.558
carrying a flask full
of crème de cassis.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.193
She was a very refined dirt bag.

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:55.627
-Oh, yes.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:57.930
-Wait a minute!
-[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.399
-But it's very funny about --
Addie --

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:02.167
First of all, here --

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:05.170
I do want to show how beautiful
my grandmother was.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.040
There she is on her wedding day
in the middle.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.810
Here she is right there.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.111
And that's you right there, Mom.

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.347
-Yeah. That's with five
of her six children.

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:15.715
-Yep. You had one more baby
after you.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:19.718
-One more baby.
-And Addie was so refined

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:21.353
and so classy,

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:23.989
and her home was filled
with beautiful antiques.

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:26.925
I assumed until
I was about 15 years old that --

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:29.161
I just assumed she was born
in England.

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:30.963
-Right.
-Do you want to tell everybody

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:32.197
where she was born?

00:05:32.197 --> 00:05:33.999
-Kansas City, Missouri.
-Yeah.

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:36.035
-But she was sent
to an English boarding school.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.704
She went to Malvern
Girls College in England.

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:40.906
And she did -- She would
pronounce "luh-bore-atory"

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:42.908
and things like --
She had airs.

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:46.312
-Yeah, she put on airs.
Now, you -- Late in life --

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:48.547
Because she spent her
later years in San Diego.

00:05:48.547 --> 00:05:50.683
You were in L.A -- You saw her
a great deal more than I did.

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:52.584
You were very close with her,
but do you have a memory of --

00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:55.387
Because she wasn't the warmest
grandmother when we were young.

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:58.590
-No, she didn't like kids
that weren't her kids.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.292
And I was her grandchild.

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:02.762
-Right, which you feel like
is close enough.

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:04.496
-Not for Addie.
-No. No.

00:06:04.496 --> 00:06:05.898
-And, yeah, but she was --

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.500
Like, you know, she would never
say "paper muh-shay."

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.869
She would say papier maché.
-Right.

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:12.538
And then she'd like...

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.441
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:18.077
-And then have a nice,
thick drink of cassis.

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:20.512
But she -- I mean,
my favorite story --

00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:23.316
She was, you know,
our grandmother.

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:24.716
She was like 83,

00:06:24.716 --> 00:06:26.686
had gone through
a couple husbands,

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:29.756
and had gone out
on a date with this guy,

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:32.191
and he was also
getting on in years,

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:35.494
and he was dropping her off,
and he asked her something.

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:37.796
I forget how he phrased it.
What did he ask her?

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:39.131
-"Would you touch it?"

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:40.932
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.932 --> 00:06:44.003
-"Would you touch it?"

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:46.472
-She did not.
-She did not.

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:49.508
-But that gentleman
would die three months later

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:53.045
and leave his house
to his housekeeper.

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.814
And everyone was like,

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:58.150
"Well, if you would've
touched it...

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.320
maybe free house."

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.756
-It was a very loving
group of people.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:07.092
How is -- How did your, uh --
What is your memory of Addie?

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:09.996
-It was different for me.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:13.765
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.765 --> 00:07:15.668
I'm gonna tell you
the first time I met Addie.

00:07:15.668 --> 00:07:19.271
Hillary and I met in college,
and I must have been about 20,

00:07:19.271 --> 00:07:22.441
and my friend Denny Miller
and I went up --

00:07:22.441 --> 00:07:23.909
We lived in Pittsburgh.

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:25.711
We went up to
Marblehead, Massachusetts,

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:27.947
this beautiful New England town

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:31.083
with a big harbor
full of sailboats.

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:34.753
And they lived in a house
that was built in the 1600s.

00:07:34.753 --> 00:07:37.689
And we come up there,
and, you know, to have a thing

00:07:37.689 --> 00:07:39.758
at the end of the summer,
a little vacation.

00:07:39.758 --> 00:07:44.296
And so I think we got off
to a bad start.

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:45.798
You know, first of all,
we mentioned --

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:47.366
She said,
"How do you like Marblehead?"

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:50.936
We said, "Eh.
No rivers, no bridges.

00:07:50.936 --> 00:07:53.372
It's not Pittsburgh.
Eh, it's all right."

00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:57.343
So, then we were sitting
in the yard and --

00:07:57.343 --> 00:08:00.346
-The garden.
-It was the garden. Pardon me.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:03.081
-If you say "papier maché"...
-You say "garden."

00:08:03.081 --> 00:08:04.917
-...you say "garden."

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:06.819
-And I'll admit.
We were 20 years old.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.854
I mean, I don't know
what she was expecting.

00:08:08.854 --> 00:08:10.656
She wasn't expecting me.

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:13.158
And, um...

00:08:13.158 --> 00:08:16.728
You know, as far as
savoir faire went,

00:08:16.728 --> 00:08:20.365
to stay with the French,
ours was only faire.

00:08:20.365 --> 00:08:22.401
And so we're sitting
in the garden,

00:08:22.401 --> 00:08:25.437
and Hillary's grandfather
came in, Addie's father.

00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:28.707
He was quite old at the time
and with a beret

00:08:28.707 --> 00:08:31.811
and some medals
from World War I.

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:33.378
And he sat down.

00:08:33.378 --> 00:08:34.880
We shook his hand,
but we didn't get up.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.683
We should have got up.
We were, you know --

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:40.353
Again, faire
on the savoir faire.

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:44.824
And so after we had
that little exchange,

00:08:44.824 --> 00:08:46.158
we went in to change for dinner,

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:48.627
and Hillary came up and said,
"You have to get out."

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:50.363
Addie threw me out
the first day.

00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:53.866
I didn't make it one day.
She threw us out.

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:57.637
And you saw the picture of her
with five of her six children.

00:08:57.637 --> 00:08:59.070
And she was the matriarch.
She was --

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:02.842
She was -- I would say she was
like Catherine the Great.

00:09:02.842 --> 00:09:05.844
Except Catherine the Great
didn't have six kids.

00:09:05.844 --> 00:09:08.113
She was more like that guy...

00:09:08.113 --> 00:09:10.615
What's his name on "Succession"?
Roy?

00:09:10.615 --> 00:09:12.084
-Logan Roy.
-Logan Roy.

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:13.619
-She was more like him.
-Oh, that's lovely.

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:15.420
-Yeah, with a lot of kids.
-He's very --

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.255
He's loving, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:09:17.255 --> 00:09:18.523
So she was -- she was the boss.

00:09:18.523 --> 00:09:21.493
But we -- you know, we --
That was a rough start.

00:09:21.493 --> 00:09:25.464
But, you know, Hillary and I've
been married over 50 years,

00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:28.300
and so, over time,
you know, maybe 10,

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.404
12 years, things got better.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:33.905
You know, the last 30 years
were pretty good.

00:09:33.905 --> 00:09:35.641
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:35.641 --> 00:09:40.345
I should note the story
about Addie's birth

00:09:40.345 --> 00:09:43.014
was a lot less dramatic
than Axel,

00:09:43.014 --> 00:09:47.720
who was my middle son who was
famously born in a lobby.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.822
And our first son
was almost born in an Uber.

00:09:50.822 --> 00:09:54.326
So Alexi was very aware
that she has pretty fast labors,

00:09:54.326 --> 00:09:57.729
and so she decided to have it
at home with a midwife.

00:09:57.729 --> 00:10:01.333
And my wife is very --
She is an alpha.

00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:04.470
She likes to plan things.
She put our two boys to bed.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:06.806
She looked at me, she said,
"My water broke."

00:10:06.806 --> 00:10:09.441
We had a midwife
come to our home,

00:10:09.441 --> 00:10:13.812
and she gave birth in our
bathtub at 10:30 at night,

00:10:13.812 --> 00:10:14.980
which was incredible.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.383
Like three pushes,
and baby Addie was there.

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:20.018
And it was amazing because,
you know, you go to a hospital,

00:10:20.018 --> 00:10:21.987
which we did for the first two,
and, you know,

00:10:21.987 --> 00:10:24.389
it feels very medical,
and this birth was very much

00:10:24.389 --> 00:10:25.691
just felt like a miracle.

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:28.828
And we had -- Addie slept
between us at night in bed.

00:10:28.828 --> 00:10:30.629
And then, when the boys
woke up in the morning,

00:10:30.629 --> 00:10:32.231
I got to go into
their room and say,

00:10:32.231 --> 00:10:35.768
"There's a surprise
for you in Mom's bed."

00:10:35.768 --> 00:10:38.570
And here is the video
of the first time

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.273
they got to hold their sister.

00:10:41.273 --> 00:10:45.411
[ Both laugh ]

00:10:45.411 --> 00:10:47.313
Mommy, get her off, get her off.

00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:48.546
-Take her off?
[ Laughter ]

00:10:48.546 --> 00:10:50.049
-Mommy.
-What?

00:10:50.049 --> 00:10:53.319
-What is that?
-That's her belly button.

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:55.921
[ Laughter ]

00:10:55.921 --> 00:10:57.189
-We took advantage
of the pandemic

00:10:57.189 --> 00:10:58.657
and the fact that you weren't --
you know,

00:10:58.657 --> 00:11:01.060
people weren't seeing you out
and about in the same way.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.495
And we decided --
It's not really --

00:11:03.495 --> 00:11:05.530
Like,
I feel like a secret baby

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.166
has to be, like, two people
that aren't together.

00:11:08.166 --> 00:11:09.568
-Yeah.
-It's a little bit

00:11:09.568 --> 00:11:11.203
less exciting when you're like,
"We had a secret baby,"

00:11:11.203 --> 00:11:12.871
and people are like,
"It's your third baby."

00:11:12.871 --> 00:11:14.940
[ Laughter ]
-No, look, trust --

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.508
That's how I feel.

00:11:16.508 --> 00:11:18.377
I mean, I don't know why
I've been keeping it a secret.

00:11:18.377 --> 00:11:19.611
-Right.

00:11:19.611 --> 00:11:21.514
Well, I appreciate you
biting the bullet.

00:11:21.514 --> 00:11:24.350
[ Laughter ]

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:28.987
And then, you know --
you know, obviously,

00:11:28.987 --> 00:11:31.890
the fact that this one
wasn't born in the lobby,

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:34.493
you know...
didn't need to run out

00:11:34.493 --> 00:11:36.161
and tell everybody, you know?
-Yeah.

00:11:36.161 --> 00:11:37.496
-Yeah. Fair enough.
Fair enough.

00:11:37.496 --> 00:11:39.598
-No Netflix special there.
-Nope.

00:11:39.598 --> 00:11:42.401
[ Laughter ]

00:11:42.401 --> 00:11:43.936
-Boy.
-I mean, it's heartbreaking.

00:11:43.936 --> 00:11:45.304
And, you know,
you don't want to blame her

00:11:45.304 --> 00:11:46.905
'cause she's just a baby.
-Yeah.

00:11:46.905 --> 00:11:48.274
-But...
-But...

00:11:48.274 --> 00:11:50.242
You know, Axel's already
paid for college, like...

00:11:50.242 --> 00:11:51.910
[ Laughter ]

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:53.746
She's got some work to do.
You know?

00:11:53.746 --> 00:11:55.047
-Yeah.
-Hey, we're gonna

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:57.416
go to commercial,
but I'm gonna show you --

00:11:57.416 --> 00:12:01.953
We're gonna go to commercial
with a video that is one

00:12:01.953 --> 00:12:05.123
that I have watched a hundred
times since we filmed it.

00:12:05.123 --> 00:12:07.459
This is Axel.

00:12:07.459 --> 00:12:08.894
Both my boys
are incredibly sweet.

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:10.228
This is Axel.

00:12:10.228 --> 00:12:11.997
We lower-thirded it
because he's a little hard

00:12:11.997 --> 00:12:13.665
to understand sometimes,
but this is

00:12:13.665 --> 00:12:16.335
his first conversation
with his baby sister.

